 That's correct! Your MATLAB code sets up the system of equations with symbolic variables "a," "b," "c," "p," "q," "r," and "x." The equations are defined as eq1, eq2, and eq3. The "solve" function is then used to find the values of "a," "b," and "c" that satisfy all three equations. Once these values have been found, you can substitute them back into the expression for "x" (eq3) to find its value. Here's how you could do it:
```MATLAB
% Given equations
syms a b c p q r x
eq1 = ap + bq + cr == 28; % a*p + b*q + c*r = 28
eq2 = a + b + c == 10;     % a + b + c = 10
eq3 = x == a + 2*b - c/3;  % x = a + 2*b - c/3
% Solve the system of equations
sol = solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], [a, b, c]);
% Extract the values of a, b, and c from the solution
a_value = sol.a;
b_value = sol.b;
c_value = sol.c;
% Calculate the value of x
x_value = subs(eq3, {a, b, c}, {a_value, b_value, c_value});
% Display the results
disp(['Solution for x: ', num2str(double(x_value))]);
```
This code will display the numerical value of "x" based on the given system of equations. Remember to assign appropriate values for "p," "q," and "r" before running the code if they are not constants.

