```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') { // Default order is ascending
    if (!is_array($set) || count($set) <= 1) {
        return $set;
    }

    $n = count($set);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $n - 1; $i++) {
        for ($j = 0; $j < $n - $i - 1; $j++) {
            if (($order === 'ASC' && $set[$j]['age'] > $set[$j+1]['age']) || ($order === 'DESC' && $set[$j]['age'] < $set[$j+1]['age'])) {
                $temp = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $set[$j+1];
                $set[$j+1] = $temp;
            }
        }
    }

    return $set;
}
```

This `smart_sort_array` function takes an associative array called `$set` as its first argument and an optional second argument `$order`, which defaults to 'ASC' for ascending order. It checks if the input is valid and then uses a nested loop for simple switching of elements based on the given order.

