 To retrieve the username from a JWT (JSON Web Token) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi project, you can use the `ClaimsPrincipal` and `ClaimsIdentity` classes that are part of the `System.Security.Claims` namespace. Here's how to get it:

1. First, ensure that your middleware or authentication filter has properly deserialized and stored the JWT token into the `HttpContext.Current.User` property as a `ClaimsPrincipal`. This is typically handled by the `JwtBearerAuthenticationMiddleware` or similar components in most common authentication scenarios.

2. In your controller action method, check if the user is authenticated using the following code snippet:

```csharp
if (User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
{
    // User is authenticated, continue with the logic below
}
else
{
    // Handle unauthenticated requests
}
```

3. Once you have confirmed the user is authenticated, extract the username claim value from the `ClaimsIdentity` object:

```csharp
string username = User.Identity.Name;
```

This will give you the username string that was included in the JWT token during the authentication process.

Here's a complete example of an action method that checks for authentication and retrieves the username:

```csharp
using System.Security.Claims;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

[Route("api/[controller]")]
[ApiController]
public class ValuesController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet]
    public ActionResult<string> Get()
    {
        if (!User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
            return Unauthorized();

        string username = User.Identity.Name;

        // Use the username for further processing, like querying a database, etc.
        return Ok($"Hello, {username}!");
    }
}
```

