 Yes, you can store and manage binary data like images in MongoDB using the GridFS specification. GridFS is not a built-in feature of MongoDB but an extension to handle large files that exceed the BSON-document size limit of 16MB. To use GridFS, you need to install a driver like `gridfs-stream` or `mongoose-gridfs-storage` for your preferred programming language. Here's a simple example using Mongoose (Node.js):

1. Install `mongoose` and `mongoose-gridfs-storage`:

```bash
npm install mongoose mongoose-gridfs-storage
```

2. Create a schema and model:

```javascript
const { Schema, model } = require('mongoose');
const gridfsStorage = require('multer-gridfs-storage');
const GridFsStorage = new gridfsStorage({
  url: 'mongodb://localhost:27017/myDatabase',
});

const imageSchema = new Schema({
  name: String,
  createdAt: Date,
});

imageSchema.plugin(require('mongoose-timestamps'));

const Image = model('Image', imageSchema);

module.exports = Image;
```

3. Use Multer with GridFS:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const multer = require('multer');
const upload = multer({ storage: GridFsStorage });

const app = express();

app.post('/upload', upload.single('image'), async (req, res) => {
  const image = new Image({
    name: req.file.originalname,
  }).save();

  res.status(200).send(image);
});

// ... other routes

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server started on port 3000'));
```

In this example, when making a POST request to '/upload' with an image file attached, the image will be saved in MongoDB using GridFS. The metadata, such as the filename and creation timestamp, will also be stored in the associated document within the 'Images' collection.

